 "news": {
  "date": "2026-10-14",
  "asof": "2026-10-15 09:36 UTC",
  "headline": "Inflation printed hot and the Nasdaq lost 1.9% \u2014 then the long end closed lower for a fourth session running",
  "standfirst": "September CPI came in +0.6% on the month and +3.7% on the year, core +0.3% and +2.6%, both above forecast \u2014 with gasoline up 9.1% on the month and 34.2% on the year, accounting for over half the monthly increase on the BLS's own reckoning. Hike odds for 28 October roughly doubled to about 50%. Shares had their worst day since July: S&P \u22121.11% to 7,718.94, its fifth loss in six sessions since the record, Nasdaq \u22121.88%, Russell \u22121.93% \u2014 and the Dow \u22120.09%. Then the bond market refused it: the ten-year spiked 10 basis points toward 5.3% and closed back at about 5.19%, while the thirty-year closed lower at 5.53%, a fourth consecutive session of the long end easing. In the book OKTA fell 6.45%, GOOG 1.68% and AMZN 1.74%, whose low of 252.66 came within 82 cents of its breakeven stop, while AAPL rose 0.50% and was the only holding to gain, on a Securities Times report that Apple has raised iPhone Duo orders 30% against Nikkei's production-cut claim. A net of -32.29, the worst day of the stretch. Heat 3.05R against 3.0R, unchanged for a twenty-fifth session. The foldable opens for pre-order on Friday.",
  "themes": [
   {
    "kicker": "What's next",
    "right": "the foldable tomorrow \u00b7 the Fed on the 28th",
    "title": "Friday is the first thing anyone can actually check",
    "items": [
     {
      "tone": "c-er",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>iPhone Duo pre-orders open</b>, 5am Pacific \u2014 BofA calls it the indicator of premium demand, and it is the first test of whether buyers were waiting for the foldable",
      "when": "Fri \u00b7 2026-10-16",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-er",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>iPhone Duo on sale</b> in the US and 70-plus countries from <b>$1,999</b>, with 28 more countries on the 30th",
      "when": "Fri \u00b7 2026-10-23",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "FOMC",
      "html": "<b>Fed decision</b> and <b>Alphabet Q3</b> on the same day \u2014 hike odds roughly doubled to about 50% on Wednesday's print",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-28",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "AAPL \u00b7 AMZN",
      "html": "<b>Apple Q4 and Amazon Q3 earnings</b> \u2014 the first iPhone 18 quarter against two contradictory supply-chain reports, and Amazon's first read on holiday demand",
      "when": "Thu \u00b7 2026-10-29",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "CPI",
      "html": "<b>October CPI</b> \u2014 the next first-tier print, with the same energy risk built into it",
      "when": "Tue \u00b7 2026-11-10",
      "kind": "calendar"
     }
    ],
    "note": "<b>Friday matters more to this book than anything else on that list.</b> <b>iPhone Duo pre-orders open at 5am Pacific on 16 October</b>, with sale on the <b>23rd</b> at <b>$1,999</b> for 256GB rising to $3,199. Two supply-chain reports now contradict each other: Nikkei says iPhone 18 Pro production is being cut on weak demand; Securities Times says Pro supply targets are unchanged and <b>Duo orders have been raised 30%</b> toward 10 million units. Bank of America, keeping Buy at <b>$370</b>, notes Pro delivery times are shorter than a year ago \u2014 which supports the weak-Pro case \u2014 and calls Friday the real indicator. Pre-order sell-through is checkable within hours, and it arrives thirteen days before Apple has to explain the quarter."
   }
  ],
  "ondeck": "<b>Friday matters more to this book than anything else on that list.</b> <b>iPhone Duo pre-orders open at 5am Pacific on 16 October</b>, with sale on the <b>23rd</b> at <b>$1,999</b> for 256GB rising to $3,199. Two supply-chain reports now contradict each other: Nikkei says iPhone 18 Pro production is being cut on weak demand; Securities Times says Pro supply targets are unchanged and <b>Duo orders have been raised 30%</b> toward 10 million units. Bank of America, keeping Buy at <b>$370</b>, notes Pro delivery times are shorter than a year ago \u2014 which supports the weak-Pro case \u2014 and calls Friday the real indicator. Pre-order sell-through is checkable within hours, and it arrives thirteen days before Apple has to explain the quarter.",
